    hero: {
      title: 'RUMMY',
      subtitle: 'Basic Rummy',
    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Rummy',
            subtitle: 'Basic Rummy',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Rummy is a classic draw-and-discard matching game. Players form melds (sets of three or four of a kind, or runs of three or more in suit) and try to dispose of all cards. Draw one, meld and lay off optionally, discard one. First to go out wins; others count deadwood points.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Rummy games first appeared in the early twentieth century. Two main theories: Mexican origin from Conquian (first attested 1852), or Asian from a Mahjong-related game such as Kun P'ai. The draw-and-discard principle with melding appears in Chinese card games from the 18thâ€“19th century. The name 'rummy' may derive from British slang ('rum' = odd), Rum Poker, or the drink.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '2â€“6 (optimal 2â€“4). Deal alternates (2 players) or rotates clockwise.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck. Cards rank A low to K high.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Deck, flat surface. Agree target score or number of deals before play.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '1. Deal 10 (2 players), 7 (3â€“4), or 6 (5â€“6) cards each, one at a time. 2. Remaining cards form the stock. 3. One card turned face-up starts the discard pile.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Going Rummy: If you go out in one turn without having melded or laid off before, score is doubled (or +10 bonus).',
                  },
                  {
                    text: "No Lay-Off Until Meld: You may not lay off on others' melds until you have melded at least once.",
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Ace High or Low: Aces can be high (Q-K-A) or low (A-2-3). Aces then count 15 in deadwood.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: "Block Rummy: Discard pile is not reused. When stock runs out and next player doesn't want the discard, game ends. Lowest deadwood wins.",
                  },
                  {
                    text: '500 Rummy: Multiple cards can be taken from discard; points for melds; play to 500.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Gin Rummy: Two players; knock when deadwood â‰¤10; no lay-off until knock.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
    description: 'Rummy is a classic draw-and-discard matching game. Players form melds (sets of three or four of a kind, or runs of three or more in suit) and try to dispose of all cards. Draw one, meld and lay off optionally, discard one. First to go out wins; others count deadwood points.',
    tags: [
